1,2 $ . Basidiocarps growing on pure dung.
1,4 $ . Basidiocarps not on dung.
2,0 $ Pileus < 10 mm when expanded; length of spores < 11 µm: $ C. miser P.Karst.
2,3 $ . Pileus > 10 mm when expanded; length of spores 10--15 µm.
3,0 $ Spores ellipsoid with average Q more than 1.4: $ C. megaspermus P.D.Orton.
3,0 $ Spores heart-shaped with average Q less than 1.4: $ C. schroeteri P.Karst.
4,0 $ Average breadth of spores < 7.8 µm; spores rounded triangular: $ C. kuehneri Uljé & Bas.________________________________________
4,5 $ . Average breadth of spores > 7.8 µm; spores on average more than 9 µm broad when rounded triangular.
5,0 $ Pileus lilaceous when young and fresh; spores 10--13 x 9--11 x 6--8 µm, 5--6 angular; pileipellis and cheilocystidia with oily granules: $ C. lilatinctus Bender & Uljé
5,6 $ . Pileus without lilaceous tinges; spores on average less than 9 µm broad when 5--6 angular; cheilocystidia without oily granules.
6,7 $ . Average length of spores < 11 µm.
6,8 $ . Average length of spores > 11 µm.
7,0 $ Spores heart-shaped in frontal view with rounded angles: $ C. leiocephalus P.D.Orton
7,0 $ Spores sub(globose) in frontal view without angles: $ C. galericuliformis Watl.
8,0 $ Expanded pileus small, up to 13(--17) mm; spores 12--17 x 11.5--16 µm: $ C. hercules Uljé & Bas
8,9 $ . Expanded pileus up to 30 mm broad; spores less than 14 µm broad.
9,0 $ Spores 10--14 x 7.5--10 µm, elongate 5--6 angular: $ C. plicatilis (Curt.: Fr.) Fr.
9,0 $ Spores 10--16 x 9--13 µm, rounded triangular: $ C. schroeteri P.Karst.
